RE-HARMONY: expose two read-only entry points in libconcord
===========================================================

Subject: [PATCH] libconcord: add read_flash_at() and read_misc_at()
Applies to: jaymzh/concordance, libconcord 1.5 (branch `main`)
License:    GPL-3.0-or-later, same as the code it modifies.
Author:     the RE-HARMONY project. Not affiliated with the Concordance
            project; this is a downstream change, not an upstream release.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
Upstream libconcord has no way to read an arbitrary flash address. Its dump
entry points read relative to `firmware_base` from `remote_info.h`, and for
arch 12 -- the Harmony One -- `firmware_base` is 0. So every call reads
address 0 and there is no route to the configuration region at 0x040000.

RE-HARMONY reads the remote's current configuration and refuses to write
anything it has not first read and diffed. Without `read_flash_at()` it
cannot read, so it cannot write, so nothing in the project works.

`read_misc_at()` is the second half: READ_MISC subtype PROGRAM (4) is the only
route that reaches the CPU's own view of memory. READ_FLASH goes through the
JEDEC driver to the external chip, which is why reading 0x020021 with it
returns what is stored in flash instead of the live keyboard port.

WHAT IT CHANGES (4 files, 4 hunks, +71 -8)
------------------------------------------
  libconcord.h    declare read_flash_at() and read_misc_at()
  libconcord.cpp  implement both. read_misc_at() refuses on Z remotes
                  instead of downcasting a CRemoteZ to CRemote
  remote.h        move CRemote::ReadMiscByte/ReadMiscWord from private to
                  public. The two MISC *writers* stay private
  remote_mh.cpp   two robustness fixes found while surveying: (a) an MH read
                  response with a single parameter is an ERROR code, not a
                  zero-length read -- reading it as a length made the caller
                  ack and then hang until the read timeout; (b) guard
                  `len >= 4` before writing the EOF marker at `rd + len - 4`,
                  which otherwise writes before the start of the buffer for a
                  hub that never reported a config length

Nothing here writes to a remote. No existing entry point changes behaviour;
the diff is additive except for the two guards above and the private->public
move.

